{% extends "guide-caution.njk" %}

{% block section_0 %}
<p>
  Bengali (বাংলা, Bangla) is spoken by approximately 230–265 million people, making it the seventh most spoken language in the world. It is the official language of Bangladesh and the state language of West Bengal (India) — with significant Bengali-speaking communities also in Tripura, Assam, and across the global Bengali diaspora.<br>
  Bangladesh's digital economy is one of South and Southeast Asia's most rapidly growing — with an expanding middle class, a large young population, and rapidly increasing smartphone and internet penetration. West Bengal and India's Bengali-speaking population represent an additional high-value audience.<br>
  This guide covers what you need to build a Bengali-language website that is technically correct, culturally resonant, and positioned to reach this fast-growing market.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_1_1 %}
<p>
  Bengali has two main national varieties, and the differences are meaningful enough that content written for one market may feel noticeably foreign to readers from the other:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
  <li><strong>Bangladeshi Bengali (Standard Dhaka Bengali):</strong> The standard for Bangladesh. Has Arabic/Persian loanwords due to Islamic influence that are less common in Indian Bengali. Uses a slightly more formal register in written business communication. This is the larger market by population and fastest-growing in digital terms.</li>
  <li><strong>West Bengal Bengali (Standard Calcutta Bengali):</strong> The standard for West Bengal and Indian Bengali speakers. Has more Sanskrit-derived vocabulary and has been influenced by the colonial Calcutta literary tradition. "Shuddho Bangla" (pure Bengali) is the formal written register.</li>
  <li><strong>Spoken dialects:</strong> Both countries have numerous regional spoken dialects (Chittagong, Sylheti, Rajshahi, etc.) that differ significantly from the written standard. Written web content always uses the written standard — not dialects.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Both variants use the same Bengali script (বাংলা লিপি) — so a single font can render both correctly. Set <code>&lt;html lang="bn"&gt;</code> or <code>lang="bn-BD"</code> for Bangladesh / <code>lang="bn-IN"</code> for Indian Bengali.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_1_2 %}
<p>
  Bangladesh and West Bengal operate largely open internet environments, but with some specific technical considerations:
</p>
<div style="background:#fff4f4; padding:16px; border-radius:8px; border:1px solid #f9a8a8; margin-bottom:24px;">
  <p style="font-weight:bold; color:#c53030; margin-bottom:8px;">❌ Common technical issues for Bengali websites</p>
  <ul style="list-style:none; padding:0; margin:0;">
    <li style="margin-bottom:8px;">· Incorrect Bengali font rendering — many generic font stacks don't include Bengali, causing the browser to fall back to system fonts with inconsistent results</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:8px;">· Heavy pages that perform poorly on affordable Android smartphones (the dominant device in Bangladesh)</li>
    <li>· Unicode encoding errors in Bengali text — Bengali script requires correct UTF-8 handling throughout the CMS, database, and server stack</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>
  Bangladesh in particular has a mobile-dominant internet landscape with meaningful network quality variation between Dhaka and secondary cities. Performance optimization is more important here than in markets with uniformly fast connectivity.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_1_3 %}
<p>
  Bangladesh and India have different legal frameworks applicable to Bengali-language digital services:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
  <li><strong>Bangladesh — Digital Security Act (DSA) and ICT Act:</strong> Bangladesh has active digital content regulations. The Digital Security Act (2018) — now being replaced by the Cyber Security Act (2023) — has provisions that can affect online content. Foreign businesses targeting Bangladeshi audiences should be aware that content deemed offensive or defamatory under Bangladeshi law carries legal risk.</li>
  <li><strong>Bangladesh — Data protection:</strong> Bangladesh does not yet have a comprehensive data protection law equivalent to GDPR, though draft legislation has been under consideration. The ICT Act includes some privacy provisions. Standard data protection best practices (clear privacy policy, minimal data collection, user consent) are advisable.</li>
  <li><strong>India (West Bengal) — DPDPA 2023:</strong> Same as Hindi market considerations — India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act applies to Bengali-language services targeting Indian users.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_2_1 %}
<p>
  Bengali-speaking audiences — particularly in Bangladesh — are rapidly adopting digital commerce, with distinct content expectations:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
  <li><strong>Local language content is scarce and valued:</strong> High-quality Bengali web content remains relatively rare compared to English or Hindi. Businesses that invest in genuinely well-written Bengali content — rather than rough translations — stand out significantly.</li>
  <li><strong>Facebook as the primary content platform:</strong> In Bangladesh, Facebook has an unusually high level of dominance for business content discovery — many Bangladeshi users' experience of the internet IS Facebook. A Facebook Business Page with active Bengali content is often more important than the website itself for Bangladeshi audiences.</li>
  <li><strong>WhatsApp and Messenger:</strong> Both are widely used in Bangladesh and West Bengal for business communication. WhatsApp has higher penetration in West Bengal; Facebook Messenger is heavily used in Bangladesh.</li>
  <li><strong>bKash (Bangladesh):</strong> bKash is Bangladesh's dominant mobile financial service — similar to M-Pesa in East Africa. For any e-commerce or payment capability targeting Bangladeshi consumers, bKash integration is essential.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_2_2 %}
<p>
  <strong>Google dominates search in both Bangladesh and West Bengal</strong> — with approximately 96–97% market share. Standard Google SEO practices apply, with Bengali-specific nuances:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
  <li><strong>Bengali content opportunity:</strong> Bengali-language web content is significantly underrepresented relative to the size of the speaker population. This means quality Bengali content can rank well with less competition than equivalent English or Hindi content.</li>
  <li><strong>Unicode keyword research:</strong> Bengali search queries use Bengali script (Unicode) — use Google Keyword Planner filtered to Bangladesh or West Bengal for accurate Bengali-language search data.</li>
  <li><strong>Voice search:</strong> Bengali voice search is growing rapidly in Bangladesh, driven by users who find typing in Bengali script on mobile keyboards cumbersome. Conversational Bengali content that reflects natural spoken query patterns is increasingly valuable.</li>
  <li><strong>hreflang for bd/in split:</strong> If serving both Bangladesh and West Bengal audiences with different content variants, hreflang tags with <code>bn-BD</code> and <code>bn-IN</code> help Google route correctly.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_2_3 %}
<p>
  Server location for Bengali-language websites depends on the primary target market:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
  <li><strong>Bangladesh:</strong> AWS does not have a data center in Bangladesh. The nearest options are AWS Singapore (ap-southeast-1) — approximately 40–60ms to Dhaka — or AWS Mumbai (ap-south-1) — approximately 50–80ms. A CDN with Bangladesh or India PoPs (Cloudflare has presence in Dhaka) significantly improves performance for Bangladeshi users.</li>
  <li><strong>West Bengal / India:</strong> AWS Mumbai (ap-south-1) is the optimal choice — standard for all India-facing deployments.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Domain choice: <code>.com.bd</code> signals Bangladeshi presence for Bangladesh-focused sites (requires local registration). <code>.com</code> is universally accepted and practical for businesses without a Bangladeshi entity.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_3_1 %}
<p>
  Bengali color aesthetics draw from both Islamic tradition (for Bangladesh) and Hindu-influenced Bengali cultural heritage (for West Bengal):
</p>
<ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0;">
  <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;">🟢 <strong>Green:</strong> The national color of Bangladesh — the flag is red and green. Green carries strong positive, patriotic, and Islamic associations for Bangladeshi audiences. A universally safe and trusted color.</li>
  <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;">🔴 <strong>Red:</strong> The second color of the Bangladeshi flag — also the dominant color in West Bengal's political culture (the left political tradition). Used for emphasis, energy, and CTAs across both markets.</li>
  <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;">⚪ <strong>White:</strong> Purity, cleanliness, and simplicity. Effective as a background color in modern Bengali digital design.</li>
  <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;">🟡 <strong>Yellow/saffron (West Bengal):</strong> Carries cultural and spiritual significance in Hindu Bengali tradition — the color of turmeric, Durga Puja, and festive celebration in West Bengal.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_3_2 %}
<p>
  Bengali uses the Bengali script (বাংলা লিপি) — a distinct Brahmic script that requires dedicated Bengali-supporting fonts. Latin fonts do not render Bengali.
</p>
<div style="background:#f1f5f9; padding:12px; border-radius:4px; font-family:monospace; margin-bottom:16px;">
  font-family: "Noto Sans Bengali", "Hind Siliguri", "Mukti", sans-serif;
</div>
<p>
  "Noto Sans Bengali" is Google's comprehensive Bengali font with full Unicode Bengali coverage and clean modern rendering. "Hind Siliguri" is a purpose-built Bengali web font from Google Fonts designed specifically for screen readability. Both are available free via Google Fonts — use Bengali font subsetting to reduce file size.
</p>
<p>
  Bengali body text works best at <strong>16–18px</strong> with a line-height of <strong>1.8–2.0</strong>. Bengali script characters have complex conjunct forms and vowel marks — generous line spacing is essential for readability. Avoid justified text alignment, which creates uneven spacing in Bengali script.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_3_3 %}
<p>
  Bengali web design norms differ somewhat between Bangladesh's fast-evolving digital market and West Bengal's established digital ecosystem:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
  <li><strong>Bangladesh — mobile-first, pragmatic:</strong> Bangladeshi web design prioritizes practical information delivery. Product pages, pricing, and contact information should be immediately accessible. The visual language of successful Bangladeshi e-commerce (Chaldal, Shajgoj, Pathao) tends toward clean, functional mobile layouts.</li>
  <li><strong>West Bengal — culturally nuanced:</strong> West Bengali audiences have strong cultural associations with literature, art, and the Rabindranath Tagore tradition. Cultural references and aesthetic quality signal sophistication for this audience.</li>
  <li><strong>Festival calendar:</strong> Durga Puja (West Bengal) and Eid (Bangladesh) are the two major festive peaks for their respective markets. Seasonal campaign design acknowledging the relevant festival builds cultural connection.</li>
  <li><strong>Facebook Page as quasi-homepage:</strong> Many Bangladeshi businesses treat their Facebook Page as the primary web presence. Your website should complement and support your Facebook presence rather than competing with it.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_4_1 %}
<p>
  Contact preferences differ meaningfully between Bangladesh and West Bengal:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
  <li><strong>Facebook Messenger (Bangladesh):</strong> In Bangladesh, Facebook Messenger is often the primary channel for customer inquiries — particularly for e-commerce and service businesses. A "Message on Facebook" CTA is often more effective than a contact form or email for Bangladeshi audiences.</li>
  <li><strong>WhatsApp (West Bengal):</strong> WhatsApp is the dominant messaging app for business communication in West Bengal and Indian Bengali-speaking areas. Essential for consumer-facing businesses targeting this market.</li>
  <li><strong>bKash button (Bangladesh):</strong> For any Bangladeshi e-commerce, bKash is the dominant mobile payment method — displaying the bKash logo and payment option is a trust and conversion signal.</li>
  <li><strong>Phone contact:</strong> Visible phone numbers are important in both markets — Bangladeshi (+880) and Indian (+91) numbers respectively build local trust.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_4_2 %}
<p>
  Mobile is the primary internet access device in both markets. <strong>Over 85% of Bangladeshi internet users are on smartphones</strong>, predominantly affordable Android devices. West Bengal's mobile penetration follows India's broadly similar pattern.
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
  <li><strong>Low-end Android performance:</strong> Entry-level Android devices (1–2GB RAM) are common in Bangladesh. Sites must load quickly and function smoothly on constrained hardware.</li>
  <li><strong>Bengali input on mobile:</strong> Bengali keyboard input on mobile is challenging for many users — some prefer typing in English and having content in Bengali for reading. Consider whether your forms and inputs need to support Bengali input specifically.</li>
  <li><strong>Facebook in-app browser:</strong> In Bangladesh, a very large proportion of web traffic arrives through Facebook's in-app browser. Test your site in Facebook's in-app browser specifically — it has rendering quirks compared to Chrome or Safari.</li>
  <li><strong>Page weight:</strong> Under 1MB is the target for Bangladesh, where data costs matter and networks can be slow outside Dhaka.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

